	"definitions": {
		"lowFinancialResilience": "Adults are described as having low financial resilience if they meet one or more of the following criteria: they are in financial difficulty due to missing payments on domestic bills and/or credit commitments in three or more of the past six months; they are heavily burdened by their existing commitments; or because they have very limited savings. Some adults exhibit two or more of these criteria.",
		"lowSavings": "Adults are considered to have low savings if they have one or more of: could only cover their living expenses for up to one week if they lost their main source of household income; would struggle with an increase of less than £50 a month in their mortgage or rent; or, if retired, strongly disagree that they have no difficulty paying for day-to-day expenses since retiring.",
		"heavilyBurdened": "Question K1: To what extent do you feel that keeping up with your domestic bills and credit commitments is a burden? Those answering that it is a heavy burden. Rebased to exclude don't know responses.",
		"inFinancialDifficulty": "Question K2: In the last 6 months, have you fallen behind on, or missed, any payments for credit commitments or domestic bills for any 3 or more months? These 3 months don't necessarily have to be consecutive months.",
		"investibleAssets": "Investible assets include all money held in cash savings products (such as savings accounts and cash ISAs) as well as savings in current accounts, plus the total current market value of any investment products held. They exclude real investments and DC pension assets. Rebased to exclude don't know and prefer not to say responses (24% in 2024).",
		"coverLivingExpenses": "Question AT4: If you lost your main source of household income, how long could your household continue to cover living expenses, without having to borrow any money or ask for help from friends or family? Rebased to exclude prefer not to say responses (5% in 2024).",
		"householdIncome": "Question D38_1: What is your total annual household income from all sources (including benefits) before tax and other deductions? Respondents choose from ranges; the chart data groups them into four bands.",
		"housingTenure": "The respondent's own housing tenure (the FCA's individual housing tenure cut), grouped as own outright, mortgage, renting and other."
	},
